  nonce: '90e5'  # 90e5
  data:  # 90e5
    kind: invariant  # 90e5
    title: "Five disagreeing vocabularies for 'inheritance edge type'; type_hierarchy ignores the registry constant, so solidity 'inherits' and ruby 'includes' get no dispatch"  # 90e5
    status: violated  # 90e5
    priority: 1  # 90e5
    parent:  # 90e5
    tags:  # 90e5
    - linker  # 90e5
    - protocol  # 90e5
    - linker_gap  # 90e5
    - dogfood  # 90e5
    isbefore: []  # 90e5
    duplicate_of: []  # 90e5
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 90e5
    pr_ref:  # 90e5
    description: "**The same defect audit-findings 0018 fixed for `Symbol.kind`, one layer over — on `Edge.edge_type`.**\n\n`edge_types.py` exports a registry-backed grouping:\n\n    INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES = frozenset({\"extends\", \"implements\", \"inherits\"})\n\n`slice.py` and `ranking.py` consume it correctly (both carry a \"WI-lobif: comes from the registry\" comment). Nobody else does. Measured across the tree, there are **five different vocabularies for \"inheritance-ish edge\"**, all disagreeing:\n\n    edge_types.INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES   {extends, implements, inherits}      the registry\n    cli.py:6697                          {extends, inherits, implements}      complete hand-rolled COPY\n    linkers/_transitive_bases.py:46      (\"extends\", \"implements\")            missing `inherits`\n    linkers/inherited_calls.py:137       (\"extends\", \"implements\", \"includes\") has `includes`, missing `inherits`\n    linkers/type_hierarchy.py            inline `== \"extends\"` / `== \"implements\"`, missing both\n\n**Measured consequence** (from the new interface-dispatch parity column):\n\n* **solidity** — `contract Square is Shape` emits `inherits: contract:Square -> interface:Shape`. Everything is correct: both types emitted, both methods emitted. `type_hierarchy` never sees the edge because it branches inline on two literals and never consults the registry constant that *does* contain `inherits`. Solidity gets no dispatch.\n* **ruby** — `include Shape` emits `includes: class:Square -> module:Shape`. `includes` IS registered on `AXIS_RELATIONSHIP` but is **absent from `INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES`**, so every consumer built on that constant is blind to Ruby mixins too — not just `type_hierarchy`. Ruby gets no dispatch.\n\nNote the two are different sub-cases: solidity's value is in the registry grouping and the consumer ignores the grouping; ruby's value is not in the grouping at all. Fixing only the consumers leaves ruby broken; fixing only the grouping leaves solidity broken.\n\n**Prior art for the remedy, in this repo, on the sibling axis.** ADR-0023 §3 already instructs: *\"Consumer-side sets that need a subset of edge types should call `edge_types_on_axis(...)` instead of maintaining their own list.\"* That instruction exists and is being ignored by four of five sites. Audit-findings 0018 + PR #130 built exactly this for `Symbol.kind` — a registry-backed predicate plus a linter with `depends_on`-aware exemptions. The same shape applies here and the linter is largely reusable.\n\n**Open question that needs a ruling, not just a sweep:** is `includes` a member of the inheritance family? A Ruby `include` is how Ruby gets polymorphism, so for *dispatch* purposes it behaves like `implements`. But `INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES` may have consumers for which mixin-inclusion is genuinely not inheritance (`slice.py`'s structural expansion, `ranking.py`'s centrality). Rule per-consumer before widening the shared constant — widening it blindly is the mirror-image mistake of the hand-rolled copies.\n\n**Do NOT fold this into INV-tihim.** INV-tihim is now correctly scoped to the per-language causes; this is the cross-cutting consumer-vocabulary cause behind two of its cells. Filed separately so it can close on its own evidence."  # 90e5
    fields:  # 90e5
      statement: "Every consumer that needs the inheritance-edge family resolves it from the registry (edge_types.INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES / edge_types_on_axis), so all consumers agree and a language whose analyzer emits inherits or includes gets the same dispatch treatment as one emitting extends or implements."  # 90e5
      root_cause: "The fact is recorded five times and the copies disagree. edge_types.py exports INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES = {extends, implements, inherits} and only slice.py and ranking.py consume it; cli.py hand-rolls a complete copy, _transitive_bases.py omits inherits, inherited_calls.py has includes but omits inherits, and type_hierarchy.py branches inline on two string literals and consults the registry not at all. ADR-0023 section 3 already instructs consumers to call edge_types_on_axis instead of keeping their own list; four of five sites ignore it. Separately, includes is registered on AXIS_RELATIONSHIP but is absent from the INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES grouping, so ruby mixins are invisible to every consumer of that constant."  # 90e5
