  clock: 3  # 11ba
  nonce: 11ba  # 11ba
  message: "HUMAN RULING (2026-08-20 interview) ON THE OPEN QUESTION THIS ITEM RAISES. The item asks whether 'includes' (ruby mixin) is a member of the inheritance family, and states that the question blocks a sweep. The owner's ruling is a DELEGATION, not an answer: in due course, investigate the question carefully -- in light of the spec, the ADRs, the pertinent commit history, the pertinent tracker discussion, and the hypergumbo spirit -- and then decide using best judgement and move forward. So the decision-gate on this item is CLEARED in the sense that it no longer waits on the owner; what it now waits on is the investigation itself, whose findings are the justification for whichever way it goes. NOTES FOR WHOEVER RUNS IT, from the adjacent work just completed. (1) The keep-distinct PRECEDENT is WI-moliz, an enrolled child of the INV-numat umbrella, closed 2026-06-30 with an explicit ruling that extends/inherits/implements/overrides 'name genuinely different relations' and that 'a consumer wanting all subclass relationships enumerates {extends, inherits} -- acceptable'. That ruling PRESUMES there is one correct enumeration; this item is the finding that there are five and that four of five consumers ignore the registry. So WI-moliz did not resolve the vocabulary problem, it handed it forward to registry discipline, and the handoff did not land. Read both together. (2) The per-consumer framing the item already names is the right shape and matches how the constructor-on-instantiates question resolved this session: the answer was NOT uniform across consumers, and the deciding evidence was reading what each consumer DOES with the value rather than what the value is called. (3) A registry-derived resolver plus a live-tree AST guard WITH A POSITIVE CONTROL is now established practice here -- call_family_edge_types() (WI-toruz/INV-lalad) and the INV-tadup fold guard are both in tree and are the pattern to copy rather than reinvent."  # 11ba
- op: discuss  # 92d5
  at: '2026-08-21T08:31:43Z'  # 92d5
  by: agent  # 92d5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 92d5
  clock: 4  # 92d5
  nonce: 92d5  # 92d5
  message: "RULING ON THE OPEN QUESTION, delegated by the owner 2026-08-20. The question was: is 'includes' a member of the inheritance family? ANSWER: NO -- and the item's framing of the question was the thing that needed correcting. This item describes 'includes' as the Ruby mixin edge type. It is not one relationship. 'includes' is a REGISTERED TWO-MEANING VALUE: its own registry entry reads 'File or class includes / sources / mixes-in another unit's ...', and it is emitted by NINE producers of which EIGHT mean file inclusion (latex, make, meson, puppet, requirements, rst, scss, twig). Exactly one, the inheritance linker, means the Ruby mixin.\n\nEVIDENCE, measured on the Rails app postal: 41 'includes' edges -- 39 class->module mixins from inheritance-linker, and 2 file->external_symbol edges from an SCSS at-include of a Sass mixin. Enrolling the bare string in INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES, which is the obvious reading of this item, would have made a stylesheet at-include, a Makefile include and a LaTeX include directive into class is-a edges inside slice expansion, ranking centrality and the dead-code ancestor walk. That is precisely the mirror-image mistake this item warned against, reached by following this item's own framing.\n\nSO THE FAMILY IS A PREDICATE OVER THE EDGE, NOT A SET OF TYPE STRINGS. is_inheritance_edge(edge_type, evidence_type) returns True for extends/inherits/implements unconditionally and for 'includes' only when evidence_type names a mixin declaration. The discriminator already existed and is registry-backed: evidence_type='ast_includes', spec text 'Edge inferred from a runtime mixin declaration (Ruby include/extend, etc.)', produced by exactly one emit site. INHERITANCE_EDGE_TYPES itself stays {extends, inherits, implements} -- membership there means 'is-a regardless of what produced it', true of those three and false of 'includes'.\n\nPER-CONSUMER, as the item required. Class A, needs the flat family, now all route through the predicate: slice.py, ranking.py, cli.py dead-code ancestor walk, cli.py test-exclusion preservation, _transitive_bases.py, inherited_calls.py. Class B, needs the family SPLIT because virtual dispatch through concrete inheritance is language-dependent while interface satisfaction is not: type_hierarchy, via inheritance_dispatch_role(). Class C, legitimately narrow and NOT a copy of the family: inheritance.py's two dedup sets name what each enclosing function itself emits, so they are correctly scoped and were left alone.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE, and it is not uniform. postal: type-hierarchy dispatches 53 -> 58. openzeppelin-contracts: ZERO delta across 516 inherits edges. The Solidity residue has a SECOND cause on a DIFFERENT axis -- solidity emits contract members as kind=function (2,025 members of type-like containers, none of kind=method) while link_type_hierarchy indexes candidates with the literal sym.kind != method, so the edge now reaches the dispatch map and finds it empty. Filed as INV-lapas rather than folded in. Note the parity matrix's own xfail reason for solidity ALREADY named both causes; INV-lapas is the half that remains.\n\nINDEPENDENT CLOSURE EVIDENCE. test_interface_dispatch_parity's ruby cell was a STRICT xfail whose reason named this item by ID. It now XPASSes, i.e. a pre-existing gate written by someone else flipped green. That xfail is retired; the solidity cell stays xfail with its reason re-scoped to INV-lapas.\n\nALSO FIXED IN PASSING, because widening the family made it reachable: link_type_hierarchy unioned its two maps with two dict.update() calls, which REPLACE the value for a shared key, so a type both extended and implemented lost one whole child list. Honest scope -- latent by construction, not observed live; all three measured repos (openzeppelin-contracts, postal, sherpa-onnx) emit zero implements edges between them.\n\nTHE GUARD. find_partial_inheritance_family_literals() fails CI on any core module enumerating part of the family by hand. It reads == CHAINS as well as literal collections, because the site that caused this defect was an if/elif on two string literals and a literal-only scanner would not have caught the bug it is named for. Verified against the real defect BEFORE the fix: it fired on build_inheritance_maps() by name. Two filters keep it honest rather than merely quiet -- a one-member mention is a lookup-table row not an enumeration (scip/edges.py maps is_implementation->implements), and the registry module may enumerate what it defines. type_hierarchy also now DECLARES solidity in depends_on, which it always handled and never declared; the guard's exemption logic reads that declaration, so the inaccurate one had been making the guard quiet for the wrong reason."  # 92d5
